WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.240
Every film has its fair share of deleted scenes
— and while most of the time they tend to

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:10.020
be inconsequential, from time to time, a movie
can actually be ruined by leaving the wrong

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.810
moments on the cutting room floor.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:17.170
Here's a look at some major releases whose
editing put a major dent in everyone's enjoyment.

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.690
The Golden Compass

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.690
After the astounding critical and commercial
success of Peter Jackson's Lord of the Rings

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:27.420
trilogy in the early 2000s, New Line Cinema
tried to recapture that glory with another

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.539
fantasy trilogy: Philip Pullman's His Dark
Materials.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.320
It didn't work, since you probably forgot
this movie ever even existed.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.550
The studio hired screenwriter Chris Weitz
to helm an adaptation of the first novel in

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:43.230
the trilogy, The Golden Compass, starring
Daniel Craig, Nicole Kidman, and newcomer

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.489
Dakota Blue Richards.

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:48.780
But it was the material that Weitz decided
to leave out of his adaptation that sealed

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.040
the franchise's fate.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:54.480
The book's fans faulted Weitz for his decision
to exclude the novel's overt themes of questioning

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.370
religion by hiding them in metaphors.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.760
The film lost the novel's essence along the
way, and was widely ridiculed for softening

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.760
a major component of the story in order to
cater to a wider audience.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.360
Furthermore, in order to accommodate a shorter
runtime, Weitz had to cut out an estimated

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.750
hour from his original screenplay — mostly
scenes featuring the story's protagonist,

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.750
Lyra.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.600
With no support from the fans and general
audiences left scratching their heads, this

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.140
would-be trilogy was unsurprisingly halted
after a single chapter.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.490
Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.810
There's no denying that the DC Extended Universe
has been successful at the box office.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.670
However, there's also no getting around the
criticisms that have greeted these movies,

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:38.119
particularly 2016's Batman v Superman: Dawn
of Justice.

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:42.689
Fans and critics alike blasted the movie for
its haphazard editing and confused plot, which

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:45.579
still resulted in a 151 minute punch-stravaganza.

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:50.640
In fact, even with over two-and-a-half hours
worth of film, many felt several plotlines

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.450
and characters were underdeveloped.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:56.701
When editor David Brenner confirmed that the
original cut was almost four hours long, it

00:01:56.701 --> 00:02:01.340
became clear that the theatrical version really
was as undercooked as it seemed.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.360
Shortly after the movie's release, the studio
released an R-rated Ultimate Edition, which

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.009
added an additional 30 minutes of footage.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.540
Critics have said the extended version is
a drastic improvement, expanding upon Lex

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.349
Luthor's role and Clark Kent's investigation.

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:18.189
Maybe Zack Snyder will learn his lesson and
make the Justice League movie the full 14-hour

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:19.650
epic we all know it needs to be.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.969
That was sarcasm by the way, that'd be awful.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:23.159
Suicide Squad

00:02:23.159 --> 00:02:27.989
After the critical disappointment that came
with Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice, fans

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:32.079
hoped Suicide Squad would be a turning point
for the DC Extended Universe.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.049
Too bad it still earned more than its share
of critical scorn.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:36.459
The unhappiest critic of all?

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:39.989
That was Jared Leto, who played the movie's
new take on the Joker.

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:43.689
Although he doesn't appear too much in the
final cut, Jared Leto claimed to have filmed

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:46.650
an entire movie's worth of footage as the
Joker.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.049
And considering that Batman's arch-nemesis
was such a big draw in the first place, plenty

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:55.269
of people were disappointed when the filmmakers
relegated him to the background — so much

00:02:55.269 --> 00:02:58.810
so that some fans have actually attempted
to sue the studio.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:03.860
Warner Bros.' extended cut of Suicide Squad
added another 13-minutes to the runtime, shining

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:07.519
more light on the relationship between the
Joker and Harley Quinn.

00:03:07.519 --> 00:03:11.519
As to whether we'll ever get to see the rest
of the Joker footage that Leto shot is a mystery

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:13.989
that not even Batman can solve.

00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:14.989
Nightbreed

00:03:14.989 --> 00:03:21.579
Based on the 1988 novel Cabal by director
Clive Barker, Nightbreed follows mental patient

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:25.519
Aaron Boone, who believes he's a serial killer,
as he runs from the police and joins a group

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:29.089
of recluse monsters known as — that's right
— the Nightbreed.

00:03:29.089 --> 00:03:33.189
Since he wrote both the source material and
the script, Barker had a clear vision — one

00:03:33.189 --> 00:03:36.120
the executives at 20th Century Fox didn't
share.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.900
They wanted a slasher film with scary monsters,
and that wasn't what Barker delivered with

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.519
his two-and-a-half hour cut.

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:45.779
In response, the studio ordered a series of
reshoots and cut almost an hour out of the

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:46.849
final product.

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:50.579
In the end, Nightbreed was a critical and
commercial failure, though it did achieve

00:03:50.579 --> 00:03:52.900
cult status among hardcore horror fans.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:57.699
It wasn't until 2014 that a director's cut
saw release, running 20 minutes longer than

00:03:57.699 --> 00:04:02.560
the theatrical version and featuring 40 minutes
of altered or unseen footage.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:03.560
Daredevil

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:08.290
Mark Steven Johnson's Daredevil from 2003
received mixed-to-negative reviews during

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:13.010
its theatrical run, but was still moderately
successful, becoming the second highest-grossing

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.010
February release at the time.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.620
Shortly afterward, Johnson put together a
director's cut, which was released the following

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.620
year.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.460
It included additional footage that had been
excised from the theatrical version, as well

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:28.520
as an entire subplot featuring a drug addict
named Dante Jackson played by none other than

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:29.520
Coolio.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.900
And, as we learned from the Sci Fi original
movie Pterodactyl, the presence of Coolio

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.259
can elevate just about anything.

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:36.310
"You know we can be famous or something."

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.180
"Can that nonsense now, soldier!"

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.360
While the director's cut went unnoticed by
most, the few who saw it agreed that it turned

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.889
what once was a mess into a compelling story.

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:51.110
Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige, who
served as producer on the film, has gone on

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:55.840
record saying, "I think…the people who had
other opinions will be won over by this new

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:56.840
version."

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.860
Perhaps if Johnson's cut of the film released
in theaters instead, moviegoers might have

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:05.290
had a different initial opinion of Ben Affleck
when Warner Bros. cast him as the new Batman.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:09.210
And maybe we'd all be waiting in line for
the latest installment of Coolio's Dante Jackson

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.259
spin-off franchise.

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:12.259
Fantastic Four

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:17.370
In 2015, Josh Trank's Fantastic Four remake
released to overwhelming critical discontent,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:22.400
with the movie winning Razzies for Worst Director,
Worst Remake, Worst Screenplay, and Worst

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:23.400
Picture.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.479
Much of the film's issues have been pinned
on Trank, though he's claimed an exceptional

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:28.509
cut of the film does indeed exist.

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:29.569
But we'll never see it.

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:33.979
One day prior to the movie arriving in theaters,
Trank took to Twitter and responded to the

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:34.979
critical backlash, saying,

00:05:34.979 --> 00:05:37.330
"A year ago I had a fantastic version of this.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.229
And it would've received great reviews.

00:05:39.229 --> 00:05:40.509
You'll probably never see it.

00:05:40.509 --> 00:05:41.509
That's reality though."

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:45.509
Although many people would consider Trank's
tweet to be the rumblings of a disgruntled

00:05:45.509 --> 00:05:49.659
director, Toby Kebbell, who plays the villain
Doctor Doom, attested to the same thing, telling

00:05:49.659 --> 00:05:54.040
The Daily Beast, "I tell you, the honest truth
is, [Trank] did cut a great film that you'll

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.040
never see.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.040
That is a shame.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.530
A much darker version, and you'll never see
it."

00:05:58.530 --> 00:05:59.759
As for what we can see?

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:02.090
The wig Kate Mara wore during the movie's
reshoots.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.110
So, you know, win some, lose some.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.250
Thanks for watching!

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.860
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.979
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love!

